  "content": {
    "id": "p19-q13-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "13",
    "prompt": "It is easy to show that $2^{10}>10^3$ and $2^{13}<10^4$. Using these two facts, determine the number of digits in the decimal representation of these numbers. For part (b), also use $2^{10}=1024$ to obtain a sharper upper bound:",
    "layout": "grid",
    "columns": 2,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p19-q13-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "$2^{22}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$7$ digits.\n\nBound the number between $10^6$ and $10^7$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}2^{22}&=4(2^{10})^2\\\\2^{22}&>4\\times10^6\\\\2^{22}&=2^9\\times2^{13}\\\\2^{22}&<512\\times10^4\\\\2^{22}&<10^7\\end{align*}$$\nSince $10^6<2^{22}<10^7$, it has $7$ digits.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 52
      },
      {
        "id": "p19-q13-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "$2^{33}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$10$ digits.\n\nUse $2^{10}=1024$ to sharpen the upper bound and obtain $10^9<2^{33}<10^{10}$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}2^{33}&=8(2^{10})^3\\\\2^{33}&>8\\times10^9\\end{align*}$$\nAs in the teaching example, $2^{10}=1024$. Therefore $2^{10}<1025$, which sharpens the upper bound:\n$$\\begin{align*}2^{33}&=8(2^{10})^3\\\\2^{33}&<8(1025)^3\\\\2^{33}&<8\\,615\\,125\\,000\\\\2^{33}&<10^{10}\\end{align*}$$\nSince $10^9<2^{33}<10^{10}$, it has $10$ digits.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 92
      },
      {
        "id": "p19-q13-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "$2^{44}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$14$ digits.\n\nUse $1.6\\times10^{13}<2^{44}<3.2\\times10^{13}$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}2^{44}&=16(2^{10})^4\\\\2^{44}&>16\\times10^{12}\\\\2^{44}&=32(2^{13})^3\\\\2^{44}&<32\\times10^{12}\\end{align*}$$\nThus $10^{13}<2^{44}<10^{14}$, so it has $14$ digits.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 52
      },
      {
        "id": "p19-q13-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "$2^{55}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$17$ digits.\n\nUse $3.2\\times10^{16}<2^{55}<8\\times10^{16}$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}2^{55}&=32(2^{10})^5\\\\2^{55}&>32\\times10^{15}\\\\2^{55}&=8(2^{13})^4\\\\2^{55}&<8\\times10^{16}\\end{align*}$$\nThus $10^{16}<2^{55}<10^{17}$, so it has $17$ digits.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 52
      }
    ]
  },
